WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.310
There's no shortage of great horror movies
out there... but there's also a graveyard

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.040
full of certified clunkers just waiting to
terrorize you with their terribleness.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.500
"You're with the witch people."

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.560
"Lady, I'm just here to buy groceries."

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:13.420
"Witch!!"

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.280
We scoured Rotten Tomatoes to find some of
the worst horror movies ever made, and they

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.300
aren't for the faint of heart.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.320
Or pretty much anyone else for that matter.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.840
Please squash Human Centipede 3

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.220
"Run, Jenny!"

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.400
"Aahh!"

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.680
Newsflash: All three installments of The Human
Centipede are absolutely awful.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.420
Unless, of course, you like movies about invasive
butt surgery.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.179
After torturing audiences with the first two
films in the series, director Tom Six returned

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:44.960
in 2015 to finish the job with The Human Centipede
3.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.240
Watching this film feels like work, but without
the paycheck or health benefits.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.480
Dieter Laser, who played the villainous surgeon
in the first two installments, inexplicably

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:57.799
returns as a new, equally hideous character:
a Texas prison warden.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.409
Meanwhile, our condolences to Eric Roberts'
career:

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.140
"What the hell is this?"

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.470
Like its predecessors, The Human Centipede
3 lacks substance, style, and any kind of

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:08.960
smarts.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.060
To put it another way: this movie will make
you feel like you're at the end of the centipede.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.860
"This is exactly what America needs."

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.720
The Happening ain't happening

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.390
Following the success of blockbusters like
The Sixth Sense and Unbreakable, M. Night

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:31.060
Shyamalan was considered Hollywood's plot-twist
king.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:36.179
But his reputation took a twisty downturn
after lackluster responses to films like Signs,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.820
The Village, and Lady in the Water.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.700
Still, the worst was yet to come.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:40.979
"Just a second"

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:41.960
"There were children in that room!"

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.140
In 2008, Shyamalan released his first R-rated
film The Happening, which starred Mark Wahlberg

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.020
as an unlikely science teacher caught up in
a biological mystery.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.680
Mayhem is sweeping the country and countless
people take their own lives, and it's up to

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:56.780
Wahlberg to figure out what's going on.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.020
And it's too bad he did.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.600
The big twist is the cinematic equivalent
of a belly flop: The population is being attacked

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.770
by the Earth's plants, which is spreading
a toxin through the air to defend themselves

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.160
against destructive humans.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.940
Unfortunately, they only got their revenge
on moviegoers.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.420
"I just wanna talk in a very positive manner.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.460
Giving off good vibes.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.580
We're just here to use the bathroom."

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.540
Blair Witch 2 is cursed

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.220
"I'm scared to close my eyes, and I'm scared
to open them."

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:32.840
In 1999, The Blair Witch Project took audiences
by storm, birthing the found footage genre.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.110
Seizing on the moment, Artisan Entertainment
fast-tracked a sequel, Book of Shadows: Blair

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:37.860
Witch 2.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.860
The movie follows a group of Blair Witch fanatics
as they venture out to the Black Hills to

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.360
explore the legend first-hand.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.960
"I thought the movie was cool."

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.870
The film abandons the tone of the first film,
opting for lots of random violence that doesn't

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.340
distract from the fact that the film is a
total bore.

00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:56.480
To make matters all the more confusing, Book
of Shadows: Blair Witch 2 doesn't actually

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.140
feature a Book of Shadows.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.080
But it does feature time travel, amateur psychics,
and this guy:

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.520
"I'm finished now."

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.060
Jaws: The Revenge totally bites

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.460
"Jaws, The Revenge.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.820
This time it's personal."

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.640
When a film's tagline is the most creative
part of a film, you know you're in dark waters.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.590
A decade after Steven Spielberg created the
summer blockbuster with Jaws, Universal released

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:28.780
Jaws: The Revenge.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.600
Currently holding a zero percent rating on
Rotten Tomatoes, the film is arguably one

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.140
of the worst movies ever made.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.890
Trying to explain the premise to the Archive
of American Television, director Joseph Sargent

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.370
can barely get the words out:

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:52.500
"That a shark… could… wreak vengeance…for
killing his cousins, or whatever they were"

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:56.960
The film stars Lorraine Gary as Ellen Brody,
the wife of the late Martin Brody, played

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.920
by Roy Scheider in the original film.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.860
Ellen becomes convinced her family is being
stalked by a vengeful shark… and she's right.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.680
Odder still, this particular shark even roars:

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.740
Creepshow 3: Just no

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.840
"Ah, ah, aaahh!"

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.320
1982's Creepshow was a delight from start
to finish.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.970
The freakily fun anthology film pairs up horror
masters Stephen King and George A. Romero

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.790
and it delivers plenty of camp, carnage, and
killer style.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:34.850
It was followed up in 1987 by the weaker but
still entertaining Creepshow 2 . And then

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.460
in 2006, Creepshow 3 happened.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.000
The film is terrible, which explains its frightening
zero percent score on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:52.960
King and Romero had nothing to do with this
shlock, so instead of demonic cockroaches

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.220
and monsters in crates, we get this…

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.640
"Professor Dayton, wait!"

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:03.140
As legendary makeup artist Tom Savini says,
Tales from the Darkside: The Movie is the

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:04.200
real Creepshow 3.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:05.600
So watch that instead.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.570
"I warned them but they wouldn't listen!"

